1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:00,440
Zip.

2
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:01,885
Two rows of tiny teeth,

3
00:00:01,635 --> 00:00:04,020
locked together in under a second.

4
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,424
It’s one of the most-used machines in your life,

5
00:00:06,174 --> 00:00:08,427
and you’ve probably never looked inside.

6
00:00:08,927 --> 00:00:10,476
This is an ordinary zip,

7
00:00:10,226 --> 00:00:11,667
on an ordinary jacket.

8
00:00:11,417 --> 00:00:12,712
Two fabric tapes,

9
00:00:12,462 --> 00:00:13,818
two rows of teeth,

10
00:00:13,568 --> 00:00:15,047
and a little slider.

11
00:00:14,797 --> 00:00:16,455
But look through the slider,

12
00:00:16,205 --> 00:00:18,717
and you’ll find a beautifully simple machine.

13
00:00:20,387 --> 00:00:21,331
Lift it apart,

14
00:00:21,081 --> 00:00:22,571
and there’s the pull tab,

15
00:00:22,321 --> 00:00:23,364
the slider body,

16
00:00:23,114 --> 00:00:24,455
the two rows of teeth,

17
00:00:24,205 --> 00:00:26,438
and the fabric tapes they’re clamped to,

18
00:00:26,188 --> 00:00:27,579
with stops at each end,

19
00:00:27,329 --> 00:00:28,967
so the slider can’t run off.

20
00:00:28,717 --> 00:00:31,203
Each tooth is clamped tightly onto a corded

21
00:00:30,953 --> 00:00:32,086
edge of the tape,

22
00:00:31,836 --> 00:00:33,438
so it can’t slip or twist.

23
00:00:34,368 --> 00:00:36,378
Look closely at a single tooth.

24
00:00:36,128 --> 00:00:37,984
Each one has a little bump on top,

25
00:00:37,734 --> 00:00:39,118
and a hollow underneath.

26
00:00:38,868 --> 00:00:40,707
And the teeth on each side are

27
00:00:40,457 --> 00:00:42,667
staggered, so every tooth faces a gap

28
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:43,727
on the opposite row.

29
00:00:44,537 --> 00:00:46,407
The secret is inside the slider.

30
00:00:46,157 --> 00:00:48,187
Its channel is shaped like a Y.

31
00:00:47,937 --> 00:00:48,707
Pull it up,

32
00:00:48,457 --> 00:00:50,739
and the two rows are guided in at an angle,

33
00:00:50,489 --> 00:00:51,827
then squeezed together.

34
00:00:51,577 --> 00:00:54,066
Each bump drops into the hollow of the tooth

35
00:00:53,816 --> 00:00:55,338
beside it, and they lock,

36
00:00:55,088 --> 00:00:56,255
one after another.

37
00:00:57,225 --> 00:00:58,657
Pull it the other way,

38
00:00:58,407 --> 00:01:00,217
and a little wedge at the top

39
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:01,668
of the Y prises them apart,

40
00:01:01,418 --> 00:01:02,475
tooth by tooth.

41
00:01:02,225 --> 00:01:04,585
It’s the same simple machine as an axe

42
00:01:04,335 --> 00:01:06,196
blade, just very, very small.

43
00:01:07,166 --> 00:01:09,456
So why can’t you just pull a closed zip apart?

44
00:01:09,206 --> 00:01:11,611
Pull sideways, and every bump is trapped

45
00:01:11,361 --> 00:01:12,366
in its hollow.

46
00:01:12,116 --> 00:01:14,016
The teeth only let go when they’re

47
00:01:13,766 --> 00:01:15,715
bent apart at just the right angle,

48
00:01:15,465 --> 00:01:17,657
and that’s exactly what the slider does.

49
00:01:18,007 --> 00:01:19,577
Some sliders even lock.

50
00:01:19,327 --> 00:01:20,861
Lay the pull tab flat,

51
00:01:20,611 --> 00:01:23,137
and a tiny pin drops between the teeth,

52
00:01:22,887 --> 00:01:25,413
so the zip can’t creep open on its own.

53
00:01:25,763 --> 00:01:26,905
And on a jacket,

54
00:01:26,655 --> 00:01:28,856
the bottom end has a pin and a box,

55
00:01:28,606 --> 00:01:30,583
so the two sides can come apart

56
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:33,537
completely, then slot back together for the next zip.

57
00:01:33,887 --> 00:01:37,507
It began with Whitcomb Judson’s clasp locker in 1893.

58
00:01:37,257 --> 00:01:40,105
In 1913, Gideon Sundbäck redesigned it,

59
00:01:39,855 --> 00:01:43,037
packing ten or eleven teeth into every inch.

60
00:01:42,787 --> 00:01:44,567
And in 1923, B.

61
00:01:44,317 --> 00:01:44,567
F.

62
00:01:44,317 --> 00:01:46,266
Goodrich put it on rubber boots,

63
00:01:46,016 --> 00:01:47,380
called it the zipper,

64
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:48,389
and the name stuck.

65
00:01:49,129 --> 00:01:50,579
Put it all back together.

66
00:01:50,329 --> 00:01:51,470
The slider guides,

67
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:52,509
the channel squeezes,

68
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:54,289
and every tooth locks into the next.

69
00:01:54,039 --> 00:01:55,755
Hundreds of tiny locks,

70
00:01:55,505 --> 00:01:57,285
closed in a single pull.

71
00:01:57,845 --> 00:01:59,373
Hundreds of tiny parts,

72
00:01:59,123 --> 00:02:01,205
locked together in a single pull.

73
00:02:00,955 --> 00:02:03,771
Now imagine hundreds of tiny parts that never

74
00:02:03,521 --> 00:02:05,995
stop moving, all powered by your wrist.

75
00:02:05,745 --> 00:02:08,635
That’s inside a Rolex, linked right below.

